00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.770
Of late, the idea of
using big data data

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.080
analytics for
cybersecurity use cases

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.250
has become prevalent across
numerous industries--

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.960
areas such as smart
grids and SCADA systems,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.230
autonomous/connected vehicles,
security operation centers,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.310
health care systems can
benefit from this intersection

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.340
of big data analytics
and cybersecurity.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.230
The pinnacle of
collaboration would

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:27.530
be prevention of damage caused
by malicious cybersecurity

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:30.620
activity due to an exceptionally
accurate prediction

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.765
of such activity
before it even occurs.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.680
The gold or oil that runs the
engine of big data analytics

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.710
anywhere in any
industry is data.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:46.470
No matter how good your
advanced analytics algorithms

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.260
are, unless they've
been enriched

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.040
with vast amounts of data,
the results they provide

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.900
will not be up to par.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.080
And this is where TELUS,
like other organizations who

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.320
have access to unique
data, has a key advantage.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.900
We carry the bulk of the
nation's communication data

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.220
across our pipelines.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.220
We have a continuous stream of
both labeled and unlabeled data

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.400
from very diverse data sources,
enabling the application

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.640
of state-of-the-art advanced
analytics techniques to detect

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.530
malicious communications.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.060
Anomalous
communication detection

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.890
can be used as a first step
for malicious communication

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:27.090
detection, and this presentation
goes to that first step

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.360
and shows how we can use
Google's tech stack to power

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.470
anomaly detection.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:37.510
So now, I'd start zooming
in on the architecture

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.080
of the anomaly detection engine
that we've deployed on-premise

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:43.010
on TELUS.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.930
So anomaly detection is one
of the several analytics use

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.330
cases running on a shared
multi-tenant Hadoop 2 data

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.250
lake at TELUS.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.420
The end goal of anomaly
detection is twofold--

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.360
one, to build baseline models
for subscriber communications,

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.050
and second, to use those
baseline models to identify

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.300
anomalous subscriber
communication

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.870
and publish them downstream
during the [? inferencing ?]

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.300
stage.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.420
And then, downstream, we have
multiple analytics components

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.430
running that pick up these
anomalous points identified

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.820
by the anomaly detection
engine and correlate them

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.410
with other alerts that we
got about the subscriber who

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.355
identified malicious
user behavior.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:32.440
On the left of
the slide, you see

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.220
the tech stack that we've
deployed the anomaly detection

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.390
engine on.

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:41.280
So we've divided the tech
stack into three swim lanes.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.430
So at the bottom, you see that
we have Yarn and HDFS deployed

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:47.550
on physical nodes.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:51.020
So we use Yarn for resource
orchestration and resource

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.320
management.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.310
We also use Yarn resource
queues for our resource quota

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:01.250
amongst various cluster tenants.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:05.575
We use HDFS for reliable
and distributed storage.

00:05:05.575 --> 00:05:06.950
And then in the
middle swim lane,

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:10.490
you see that we've deployed
Spark and multiple libraries

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:11.140
of Spark--

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.180
Spark Core, Spark
ML, Spark Streaming.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.420
And we use Spark for distributed
compute for its ability

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.390
to do compute in a
distributed fashion,

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:23.330
and for stream processing and
batch processing workloads

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:24.350
at scale.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.830
We also leverage Spark
ML quite a bit to machine

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.470
learning at scale, both
for training models and for

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:32.270
[? inferencing. ?]

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.850
And then on top, you see
in the top swim lane,

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:39.270
you see that we have various
Spark applications running

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.310
for anomaly detection
engine on our cluster.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:46.080
We also use Splunk for
visualization and dashboards.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.540
And with Splunk Query Language,
we give external teams

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.470
ability to do ad hoc queries
on the data, both raw data

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.620
and [? detections ?] that
reside on our data lake.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:06.080
So in this slide, you see
an end-to-end architecture,

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.080
pipeline architecture, for
the anomaly detection engine

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.870
that we have at TELUS.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:17.690
So the architectural diagram is
divided into three swim lanes.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:19.970
We have ingest at the left.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.710
Then we have
processing swim lane,

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.020
in which we have multiple
Spark applications running.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.010
And then we have storage,
where we use HDFS.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:33.200
So what's happening over here
is that the subscriber flow

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.160
logs is wrapped up
in syslog stream,

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:41.870
and it's being streamed
to the Hadoop cluster.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:43.520
From there on in
the Hadoop cluster,

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.100
we have featured
extraction engine

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.960
which uses Spark streaming
to pick up this data stream.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.540
It uses a fixed-size
window of five minutes

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.900
to create mini
batches, and then it

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.390
does additional processing
of those mini batches.

00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:03.440
It does feature extraction,
it does feature enrichment,

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:05.240
and then it does aggregation.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.130
And that process batch is then
written to HDFS for storage.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:13.700
And you can see in number
two, in label number two.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:15.710
And then what's
happening downstream

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.900
is that the modeling
engine essentially

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:20.240
picks up that processed batch--

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.310
and you can see that
in number three--

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.160
and it applies k-means
clustering on it.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:28.960
It does distributed
k-means clustering

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.950
and build clusters
for user behavior,

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:35.000
for user communication behavior.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.880
And it extracts other material
about those clusters as well.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.430
It uses cluster mean, cluster
variance, cluster density,

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:45.740
and all of that metadata,
the clusters that we

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:50.720
have identified in this stage,
are written to HDFS or storage.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.320
Then downstream, what
happens is that you

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:57.700
see in the anomaly
detection engine,

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:02.180
we fetch those clusters that
were identified that were built

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.130
by the modeling engine, and also
the batches that were processed

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:11.523
by the feature extraction engine
where features were extracted.

00:08:11.523 --> 00:08:13.190
Those batches and the
models are fetched

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:15.650
by the anomaly detection engine,
and the anomaly detection

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:19.250
engine applies models
to those process batches

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.240
to identify anomalous
communication behavior.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.860
And the way it identifies
anomalous communication

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:26.660
behavior is by
taking into account

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.980
all the metadata that we
extracted about the cluster.

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:31.000
We use cluster centroid.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:35.480
We use cluster mean, cluster
variance, cluster density.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:39.049
And the anomalies
are a function--

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:41.270
is a function of all
these parameters.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.179
And once the anomalies
are identified,

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:45.710
they are stored to
HDFS and are also

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:48.770
published downstream,
where, as I said before,

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:51.870
the downstream analytics
components used

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.890
those anomalies
identified and correlate

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:57.260
them with other alerts
for the subscriber

00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:02.050
to identify malicious
user behavior.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:03.510
So why did we--

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:06.380
what prepared us to use GCP?

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:10.620
So we are a small
team, and what we

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.530
find is that our
time not only goes

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.070
into analytics and to developing
cybersecurity analytics,

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.710
but quite a bit of
time is also spent

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.950
on infrastructure
troubleshooting, infrastructure

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:24.460
management.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:28.250
And that's where we
want to leverage GCP's

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.570
serverless architecture
capabilities,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:33.870
so that we can be hands-free
from the infrastructure

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.810
management and infrastructure
troubleshooting issues.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:40.170
With state-of-the-art
services that GCP provides,

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:43.890
like Dataflow and
Google BigQuery,

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.140
both of them are
serverless services.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:50.970
We want to focus on our
core value prop, which

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:53.850
is cybersecurity analytics.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:59.550
Additionally, Google BigQuery
is a state-of-the-art query

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.000
engine.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.410
It's blazingly fast on
large amounts of data,

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:07.890
and it brings a lot of machine
learning capabilities out

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:08.760
of the box.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:14.200
So we want to tap into BigQuery
to create models for us

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.500
to do faster iteration
on model-building.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.500
And that allows us to
move at a fast speed.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.110
Additionally, Google
BigQuery actually

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:27.810
has a dead simple SQL interface
which a lot of data scientists

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.150
are familiar with, and that
simplifies our code quite a bit

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:35.140
and, again, allows us
to do model-building

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:38.070
at a very fast pace.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.470
And the code that we have,
because it's SQL code,

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:42.390
is easier to maintain as well.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.130
And it removes
all the complexity

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:47.700
that we have as a
result of Spark ML.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:54.170
And then, by having our
data in Google BigQuery,

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:55.830
it enables AutoML.

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:57.930
So AutoML essentially
allows you to build

00:10:57.930 --> 00:10:59.880
baseline models very quick.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.340
And that feature allows
us to test our own machine

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.112
learning models and
benchmark the performance

00:11:05.112 --> 00:11:06.570
of our own machine
learning models.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:15.090
And the elastic architecture
capabilities in Google and GCP

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:18.300
allows us to have
streamlined resource usage

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:22.340
and have a better cost
model for our pipeline.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.950
So essentially, this
is the architecture

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:28.790
that we landed in our GCP pilot.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:31.340
Essentially, when
you look at it,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.560
it's very similar
to the architecture

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:35.990
that we had on-prem for our
anomaly detection engine

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:37.190
pipeline.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:39.440
There are still
three swim lanes.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:42.410
It's ingest, its process,
and the ingest swim lane,

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.600
the process swim lane,
and the store swim lane.

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:46.490
But what we have
done over here is

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:52.940
that instead of using
Hadoop tools and frameworks,

00:11:52.940 --> 00:11:57.010
like Spark, and
Spark ML, and HDFS,

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:00.260
we've gone entirely
Google-native.

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:02.530
We're using Dataflow and Beam.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:05.030
So Beam is a
vendor-agnostic framework.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.660
We're using Dataflow,
which is serverless.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:08.660
We're using Pub/Sub.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:09.750
We're using Cloud Storage.

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:11.420
We're using BigQuery.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:14.150
BigQuery essentially is acting
as a data warehouse for us

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:18.390
and allows us to
search [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:19.920
at really fast speed.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.490
Also, BigQuery provides us with
machine learning capability

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:25.700
as I mentioned, and it allows
us for faster model-building

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:29.000
and simplifies our
machine learning code.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:31.880
So with that, I would
actually pass on to Masud,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.280
we can discuss the intricacies
of this architecture.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:36.440
MASUD HASAN: All
right, thanks, Abdul.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.900
So if you look at this
internal architecture,

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.460
let's dig a little bit more.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.585
So first thing is your
ingestion trigger.

00:12:43.585 --> 00:12:44.960
So if you look at
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.088
ingest architectures, we have
two different sources of data

00:12:48.088 --> 00:12:49.130
that we are dealing with.

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:51.710
One is Cloud Storage, and
the other one is Pub/Sub.

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:55.190
Now, for production
settings, you somehow

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:58.730
need to pass those data to the
storage and the Pub/Sub topic,

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:00.290
either through
[INAUDIBLE] plugging,

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.540
or through [INAUDIBLE] connect.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.800
Now, what we did for our POC,
because we could not get access

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.330
to all the production
data because

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:11.600
of the sensitive information,
we created a tool

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.720
using open source
Java Beam, which

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.260
we could simulate to create
250,000 messages per second

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:19.708
in a Pub/Sub topic.

00:13:19.708 --> 00:13:21.500
And I'm going to get
into a little bit more

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:22.780
on details on that.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:26.680
But now that we have all
the data in a Pub/Sub topic,

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:29.420
the second step
would be to process.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:32.270
And we have built
a Dataflow pipeline

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:36.350
that aggregates those
data in a fixed window

00:13:36.350 --> 00:13:41.660
and then do a batch insert for
a partition table in BigQuery.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:44.900
If there is any outliers found,
we also stream those data

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:47.660
to a different table on BigQuery
so that further analysis

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:48.830
can be done.

00:13:48.830 --> 00:13:51.530
Now, a lot of those logics
and you know everything that's

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:55.460
happening within that
process, we pretty much

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:57.500
rebuild it from scratch.

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.170
If you remember when Abdul
mentioned about the Spark,

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:03.230
this is actually built
on Apache Beam Java

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.590
SDK with a serverless
architecture

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:07.370
on top of XULRunner.

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:09.770
Now, the third thing
was, so now the data,

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:11.630
we have it on BigQuery.

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:14.900
What we wanted to do
is to be able to see

00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.050
how easily we could train them.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.940
And using a BigQuery ML
[INAUDIBLE] training model,

00:14:19.940 --> 00:14:25.460
we could easily train them,
and also to normalize.

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:28.640
In terms of how to make sure
that it's used in a production

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:31.550
environment, what
we did is we put all

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:38.200
the [INAUDIBLE] statement for
the QML in a stored procedure.

00:14:38.200 --> 00:14:40.370
And when you have usage
within [? scheduled ?] query

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:43.220
within BigQuery, now
you could literally

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.440
retrain the data
in either ad hoc,

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.480
or also whenever you
wanted to use it--

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:51.470
let's say in a scheduled manner.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:55.910
Now, in addition to this,
you also see the Cloud DLP.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:58.700
As I say there, may be
some sensitive information

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:00.950
on the log, so we
also tried to use

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:05.150
Cloud DLP for the detection,
for any sensitive information

00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.420
like IMSI number
or subscriber ID.

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:10.230
We automated the
whole process using

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:12.960
CloudScale for CICD purposes.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.620
Now, for the next few slides,
what we wanted to go through

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:18.720
is some of the challenges we had
to building this architecture.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:23.600
So let's take
[? it in. ?] So as I said,

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:26.020
we needed to find a
tool that we could

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:29.590
use to leverage for simulation
of production data load.

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.230
Now if you look at what's
inside this Dataflow pipeline,

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:35.950
it actually uses a open
source JSON library.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.590
On the right-hand side,
you could see a JSON schema

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:39.800
that I have.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:41.740
And if you look at the
schema that we have,

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.630
it's very similar to
the NetFlow log schema.

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:46.990
So for example, you have
fields like subscriber ID,

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:49.090
source IP, destination IP.

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:50.635
And for each one of
those attributes,

00:15:50.635 --> 00:15:53.210
it's associated with a function.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:56.410
So for example,
source IP has IPv4.

00:15:56.410 --> 00:16:00.730
What that would do is it would
generate a random IPv4 address

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:02.450
for source IP.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:05.200
Now if you look at a
lot of those fields,

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.090
like a long and
integers, you could also

00:16:07.090 --> 00:16:08.950
set up the range
for those [? both ?]

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:11.990
and the different
attributes that we have.

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:13.720
Now, the challenge
was, when you use

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:17.890
that, when you use that library
within the Dataflow pipeline,

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:21.640
we just need to make sure that
we have put enough workers so

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:25.480
that we could reach to our
target message per second goal.

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:28.060
So for our case,
what we noticed was

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:31.810
it was around 3,000 messages
per second [INAUDIBLE]..

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:34.470
So we added around 30
N1 standard for workers,

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:38.480
and it could easily reach out
to that production volume.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:41.950
There's another challenge that I
just quickly wanted to mention.

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:46.090
If you look at the
schema, it's very random.

00:16:46.090 --> 00:16:47.990
But we cannot be
completely random.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:51.040
So for example, [? fit ?]
like destination IP,

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:52.670
we needed to make
sure that because we

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:56.080
are going to do a group by
on destination IP, somehow,

00:16:56.080 --> 00:17:00.470
we limit the number of IPs
that we're going to test it on.

00:17:00.470 --> 00:17:02.950
So this tool allows
us to be very flexible

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:04.859
and create function like Submit.

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:07.089
Or also, if you
think about, end time

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:09.069
has to be after start time.

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:11.319
So those kind of
features we could easily

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:15.760
build in so that our data
is very production-like.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.430
So now that we have all the
information or all the data

00:17:18.430 --> 00:17:21.700
published on the
Pub/Sub topic, this

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.470
is where all the fun starts.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:28.000
We have built a, I would
say, pretty complex Dataflow

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.240
pipeline, pretty much
simulated whatever the logic

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:34.090
that we have on
on-prem within a week.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:36.970
Now, one of the
challenges we have to face

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:41.950
is how scalable this would be
and how fast we could actually

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.730
aggregate those data.

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:46.340
So if you look at the
production volume,

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:47.950
we're looking at
around 20 terabytes

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.560
of data, which is
250,000 messages

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:53.600
per second translates to 20
terabytes of data every day.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.210
And if you have a
10 minutes window,

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.370
you're looking at
150 gigabytes of data

00:17:57.370 --> 00:17:59.250
accumulated within that period.

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:01.600
And we are
aggregating those data

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:05.810
so that it doesn't have
a system lag in it.

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:07.880
So the way we did this is--

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:10.030
and it's a very simple process--

00:18:10.030 --> 00:18:14.650
by using Java Beam inbuilt
transform called JsonToRow.

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:18.700
So think about, as we are
getting all the JSON rows,

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:23.175
we convert them using inbuilt
function called JsonToRow.

00:18:23.175 --> 00:18:24.550
Which is really
good, because now

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:28.450
that we have row object or row
type, what it can do on row

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.150
is every row is
associated with a schema.

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:35.050
And it could group by to the
field name or the column name

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:38.600
very easily, and also add a
deaggregated function do it.

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:40.990
So for our use
case, it was more,

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:43.360
so you're going to
group by subscriber ID,

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:46.930
and you're going to group
by the destination subnet.

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:48.700
And then you apply
aggregate function

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.483
like min, max, average.

00:18:50.483 --> 00:18:52.150
So if you look at the
number of features

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.430
that we have, it's pretty
much we're aggregating data

00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:56.890
in that window, and
we're trying to find out,

00:18:56.890 --> 00:19:00.490
what is the min/max average for
transmission receiving bytes?

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:01.990
What was the duration?

00:19:01.990 --> 00:19:04.090
What is probably the
proximate count or number

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:07.040
of IPs within that time frame?

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:09.250
So if you look at on
the screenshot we have,

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:11.755
you're looking at estimated
size of only 11 gigabyte, which

00:19:11.755 --> 00:19:13.348
is a very small window.

00:19:13.348 --> 00:19:15.640
And after aggregation, you
could see that it comes down

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:19.010
to close to only 1 gigabyte.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:21.590
So the key thing was that
hey, it was really scalable,

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:26.950
and it was really easy to build
such a pipeline without just

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:30.520
using the inbuilt transform
and let the Dataflow handling

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:34.030
all the scaling issues for us.

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:36.070
So let's go to the next one.

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:39.725
So now that we have data on--

00:19:39.725 --> 00:19:41.100
so we are using
a partition table

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:44.530
because it's a 20 terabyte
data combination every day.

00:19:44.530 --> 00:19:46.500
And the reason we're
using a data partition,

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:50.760
because when you retrain those,
use those features to retrain,

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:53.760
we don't want to go back
to all the historical data.

00:19:53.760 --> 00:19:55.960
We probably only care
about the last few days.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.400
And that's why we're
using the data partition.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.250
And it was super simple
for us to do, just

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:04.200
to use a SQL statement.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.240
You could just create a
k-means clustering now

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.740
that you have your data
on a partition table.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:11.670
And within 15
minutes, we actually

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:14.010
trained close to
terabyte-scale data,

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:17.470
and it's completely segmented
with different clusters.

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:19.200
And it also allows
us to evaluate.

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.540
So for example, we
wanted to it try out

00:20:21.540 --> 00:20:23.280
with different
numbers of clusters,

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:25.240
what the DB index looks like.

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:27.610
And we found our sweet
spot was number of cluster

00:20:27.610 --> 00:20:30.673
when it was used with four.

00:20:30.673 --> 00:20:32.090
So now that the
data is segmented,

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:34.240
we're actually not done yet.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:37.350
We have to do a little
bit of normalization.

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:39.540
Now, the way we did
normalization, so now

00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:42.950
think about all the
central cluster we had.

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:46.610
And we went through each cluster
and found all those points.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:48.300
And using standard
deviation function,

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:51.190
we just created a
normalized distance.

00:20:51.190 --> 00:20:52.840
Now, that is the
normalized distance

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:55.990
that we're going to use later
for [? outlier ?] prediction,

00:20:55.990 --> 00:20:58.560
online prediction.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.350
Now, another
challenge for this one

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:04.310
was how soon you're
going to retrain, right?

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:08.480
So using a scheduled query,
along with the stored

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.750
procedural multi-statement
SQL support,

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:14.422
we just save the query and
re-run when you want it to be.

00:21:14.422 --> 00:21:16.130
And we could also do
the version control,

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.230
so that if you wanted
to change some schema

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:20.630
or change some of
the features, we

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:23.070
could easily do that as well.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:24.780
So let's go to the next one.

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:28.130
So now that we have
our normalized data,

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:29.660
what we did is--

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:32.180
so for BigQuery ML, one of the
things that you have to know

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:33.680
is that you could
actually import it

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:35.180
as a TensorFlow model.

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:37.880
And you could import
it on a GCS bucket.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:40.280
We used a very similar approach.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:44.480
What we did is we directly
query our normalized table

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:48.360
from Dataflow when it starts
up and use it as a side input.

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:50.360
So now that we have those
normalized information

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:54.260
available, and side input only
operates within the 10 minutes,

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:57.280
the 5 minutes aggregation
window that we have,

00:21:57.280 --> 00:21:59.810
so it's going to be
refreshed automatically

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.770
as our pipeline process data.

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.290
So if you recreated
the model, you

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:06.057
have a new normalized
factors, Dataflow pipeline

00:22:06.057 --> 00:22:08.390
should be able to see, is
whether they're actually doing

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:12.030
any downtime or [INAUDIBLE].

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:14.550
Now, how we are actually
doing the outlier detection?

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.140
And it's a two-step process.

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:19.050
We used Apache
Commons map functions,

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:21.450
just calculating
distance function

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:23.040
that we could easily leverage.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:25.890
So the step one was,
we needed to find out,

00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:27.930
now that we are getting
all the input vectors

00:22:27.930 --> 00:22:31.260
data, so step one
was to find out who

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:33.810
is the closest centroid, right?

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:36.420
Which is the closer centroid
for this input vector?

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:38.730
Once we know that centroid
information, based

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.570
on the normalized distance that
we have calculated earlier,

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:42.900
we put it together.

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:46.290
We put them in a
standard scoring system,

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:48.468
and if it's three standard
deviation above mean,

00:22:48.468 --> 00:22:50.010
or two standard
deviation above mean,

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:52.950
we know that that could be a
good candidate for outlier.

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:57.460
So if you look at the diagram
I have on the right-hand side,

00:22:57.460 --> 00:22:59.910
so we have three different
clusters showing,

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:02.760
and you could see that
there is one point which

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:04.860
is way farther than
our center point,

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.690
or the standard
deviation distance,

00:23:07.690 --> 00:23:10.980
but there is also a normal point
which is within the boundary.

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:14.030
So that's how we know if
it's an outlier or not.

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:16.540
Now, finding a single
outlier may not be enough.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.785
We are trying to look
for a pattern of outliers

00:23:18.785 --> 00:23:20.910
to be able to see that,
hey, there may be something

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:23.940
going on in terms of security.

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:26.090
Now, another screenshot
that I have right

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:29.340
now, you could see that
for the feature extraction,

00:23:29.340 --> 00:23:31.090
it's really great.

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:33.330
So we started the
features, and we're

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:35.070
going through the DLP transform.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:38.340
And then, we put it back
inside to our partition table,

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:41.430
but with the same feature
extent also branched out

00:23:41.430 --> 00:23:43.920
to our anomaly detection
transform, which

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:46.830
is getting all the anomalies
data as a side input.

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:48.845
And then within the
anomaly detection,

00:23:48.845 --> 00:23:50.220
we are doing those
two operations

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:51.870
that I just mentioned.

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:53.760
And then we stream
those data it found,

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:56.510
we stream those data
to the outlier table,

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:59.010
hoping that, hey, someone is
going to start looking at them,

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:02.658
and to find hey, if it's
really a risk or not.

00:24:02.658 --> 00:24:05.190
OK, so in summary,
you can see we

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:07.710
have built an end-to-end
serverless anomaly detection

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:10.090
solution in less
than a full week

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:14.130
that successfully processed
250,000 events per second using

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:18.030
Dataflow, which comes down to
around 20 terabytes of data

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:19.920
daily accumulation of data.

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:24.000
We have trained and evaluated
a terabyte-scale data

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:28.420
by using BigQuery ML
in less than 15 minutes

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:31.390
and found outliers
in near real-time.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:34.160
If you're more interested to
learn about this solution,

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:36.283
please check out the
detailed link in here.

00:24:36.283 --> 00:24:37.950
Hopefully, you'll
find this talk useful,

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.470
and thanks for listening in.

